Article 2

Protection of women and children from acts of violence is carried out based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of victims;b. justice and gender equality;
c. local wisdom;
d. non-discrimination;
e. best interests of women and children;
f. active participation; andg. restoring the situation.

Article 15

The handling of women and children victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 14 paragraph (1) letter a is carried out by:
a. hospitals;
b. community health centers;
c. Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children; d. Safe houses;
e. Women Crisis Centre;
f. Marriage Advisory, Guidance and Preservation Agency; g. Police;
h. Prosecutor's Office;
i. Courts;
j. Ministry of Law and Human Rights;
k. Legal Aid Institutions; and
1. Other Institutions.

CHAPTER II

FORMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 5

Forms of acts of violence include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect; ande. exploitation.

Article 7

Women and children from acts of violence receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity respected as human beings; b. the right to recovery of health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality of their identity;
f. the right to restitution;
g. the right .....

7 -
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right of children to education;

the right to receive facilities in the judicial process; j. the righ  and
k. the right to assistance.

Article 12

In order to prevent violations of the rights of women and children from acts of violence, the community: . . . a. actively participates in monitoring various conditions that occur in its environment that may lead to violations of the rights of women and children;
b. strengthens the role of community organizations, religious organizations and the family environment; andc. prevents unregistered marriages and child marriages.

Article 21

(1) Social rehabilitation services as referred to in Article 20 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:
a. assist victims in recognizing their problems and finding effective ways to overcome them independently;
b. empower victims to determine their future; c. provide moral support for victims in facing the legal process they are undergoing; andd. make victims feel accepted in the community environment. (2) Counseling services are conducted by social rehabilitation officers who are gender responsive, consisting of:a. counselors who have obtained certificates;
b. psychologists; andc. psychiatrists.
3. Counseling services are conducted in places that guarantee a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from victims. (4) The stages of counseling services include:a. initial counseling;
b. follow-up counseling;
c. case discussion; andd. site visits.

